WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.529
Let's talk about Chile.

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.419
Why is Milton

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.279
Friedman tied so tightly with the

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.409
Pinochet regime, which was a

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.659
repressive, tyrannical

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.509
regime that ultimately,

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.519
you know, after that period

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.619
was over, Chile was for many

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.479
years the the kind of best

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.279
performing economy in Latin America.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.319
But what did Friedman do?

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.209
And is that right to link him

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.589
to the Pinochet regime?

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.089
Yeah, it's an interesting question

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.069
because he didn't spend very much

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:29.969
time in Chile.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.949
So the kind of story starts

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.169
with the United States government

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.170
funding

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.099
students to come and study from

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.959
Chile at the University of Chicago,

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.359
which was part of a broader kind of

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.429
effort to spread American ideas

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:45.389
during the Cold War.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.979
And so this group of students, it's

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.209
eventually about a hundred is

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:49.589
trained at Chicago.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.089
Only one of them is actually

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.289
Friedman's doctoral student.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.639
But they take classes with him and

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.159
they kind of get the whole Chicago

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.719
set of economic ideas and then go

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:58.589
back to Chile.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.449
And they really

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.279
are a minority, to say the

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.079
least, because Chile is following

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.729
import substitution

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.309
industrialization, ISI, which

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.749
creates a very large role for the

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.879
state, and it's protectionist

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.409
in order to nurture domestic

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:14.579
industries.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.829
And, you know, big companies

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.209
and the state are very much

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.609
intertwined. And these

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:21.929
they're called the Chicago Boys,

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:23.849
which is pejorative.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.559
And so they want to do, you know,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.719
sort of neoclassical economics,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.939
foreign policy, free markets,

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.249
free prices, you know, open trade.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.250
They really get nowhere.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.479
Eventually it's in I think it's in

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.059
1973 that

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.219
Salvador Allende, who's a socialist

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.989
Marxist, is elected.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.879
And then three years later,

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.139
I might be getting my exact dates

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.140
wrong. I think it's 70 he's

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.599
elected. 73 he's overthrown by

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:52.979
Pinochet in a military coup.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.629
It's bloody, it's violent.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.899
And there's a great deal of upheaval

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.549
during the Allende regime and

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.659
afterwards.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.489
And so when

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.619
Pinochet comes into power, the

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.689
inflation rate in Chile is 600%

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:07.949
a year.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.109
So it's a sort of macroeconomic

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.699
crisis and a political crisis

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:12.779
wrapped in one.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.029
And then a year in,

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.289
they've gotten the inflation down

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:17.369
about 300%.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.389
But he's still having a lot of

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:19.799
problems. And it's at that point

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.719
that the Chicago boys really

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.669
been offstage for most of this,

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.799
are able to come and get into

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.509
the regime and say, look, we have a

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.009
wholly different way to do this.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:29.849
We have a different set of

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.589
approaches. Let's do that.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.659
And as are getting, you know, this

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.879
policy considered, they basically

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.169
call Friedman and they say, can you

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.329
come in? Can you talk about

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.129
what we should do to like how we

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.419
should manage the economy, how we

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:43.529
can address inflation.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.479
So Friedman decides to come.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.849
He spent six days in the country.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.319
He meets a lot of,

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.229
you know, sort of members of the

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.329
Chilean state. He meets Pinochet.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.369
He tells him you should

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:57.749
tackle inflation.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.459
You have to do basically a shock

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:00.299
treatment.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.859
You have such bad inflation, you

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:02.939
can't wait. You can't be a

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:03.929
gradualist.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:05.489
He's is a gradualist in the United

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.829
States, but not in this

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.449
hyperinflation situation.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:10.450
So then.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.319
And he also tells Pinochet, if you

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.149
open the economy the way you will,

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:16.289
you want to eventually you're going

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.119
to have to liberalize.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.829
And from the accounts we have,

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.179
Pinochet is basically like, isn't

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.099
this going to cause too much social

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.029
pain and disruption?

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:25.409
And Friedman says, you kind of have

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:26.219
no choice.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.139
And that's the message he gives to

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.159
the country more broadly.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.439
And so I have I found a travelog

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.269
where he writes all his like

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.949
he the few days after he got back

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.149
from Chile, he wrote all this down.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.229
So you have a really good sense of

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.759
what his impressions were.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.619
We can't be exactly sure what he

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.329
said because it's a censored press.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.979
But most of his talks were like

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.120
pretty boring, like,

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.029
you know, monetary policy.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.889
At any rate, that is

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.319
the policy that's put in place.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.209
And so Friedman really puts his

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.339
kind of imprimatur on the

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.959
market based reforms.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.239
And he doesn't view this as

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.579
advising, you know, a politician

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:03.719
with whom he's aligned.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.549
It's it's not as if he agreed to be

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:07.379
on the Council of Economic Advisors

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.299
of Pinochet because he supports

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.349
his plan.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:11.849
You know, he's done it's it's the

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.979
context is American economists

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.809
flying around the world telling

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.429
socialist countries like "become

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:18.338
more capitalist". Right.

00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:20.458
It's pretty a common move.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:22.589
And so then he leaves

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.569
and a couple it's not too

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.699
long after that one

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.979
of Pinochet's leading opponents

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.479
who's an exile in the United States,

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:30.784
is assassinated.

00:04:30.785 --> 00:04:32.512
By Pinochet?

00:04:32.513 --> 00:04:34.769
By Pinochet.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.179
You know, they have this kind of

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.189
secret police in a car bomb

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:39.539
on the streets of Washington, D.C.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.159
It's completely shocking.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:42.569
And then just a couple weeks later,

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.029
Friedman gets awarded the Nobel

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:46.859
and the man who was assassinated

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.839
was quite critical of Friedman and

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.909
interpreted Friedman as a supporter

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:52.949
of Pinochet and even thought

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:53.999
he was part of this kind of

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:54.989
architect of the coup.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.199
So then he's assassinated.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.569
Then the Nobel Prize happened.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.609
So the result is just this.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:01.739
All gets mixed together and it comes

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.839
to seem as if the Nobel Prize

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:04.859
Committee and the sort of

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.929
industrialized West has

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.239
decided to approve and endorse

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.799
the Pinochet regime.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.109
And so that's really a framing

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.189
that's put on it

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.909
by, you know,

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.739
Chilean exiles

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:21.517
and who are very angry with Friedman

00:05:21.518 --> 00:05:22.679
and feel like he should just have

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:23.669
steered clear of the country

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:24.990
entirely. So it becomes

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.019
just a real it's a real

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.669
controversy. It's very painful for

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.049
Friedman. And over time, he'll

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.119
realize he has to speak

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.099
out more forcefully about

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:36.359
political freedom.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:37.949
You know, his remarks previous to

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:39.359
that are really all about economic

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:40.859
freedom. And eventually he kind of

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:42.089
realizes I have to talk about

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:43.109
political freedom, too.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.339
So you'll see a shift.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:47.879
One more thing. I would say that you

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.039
and I chatted about briefly offline

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.409
is that, you know, Friedman doesn't

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.089
come out in support of the regime,

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:55.320
but there are some other,

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.699
you know, economists, most notably

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.439
F.A. Hayek, who offer more

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.329
justifications and defenses of

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:04.619
the regime. And I think in the kind

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:05.669
of public memory,

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.309
Friedman gets kind of lumped into

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:09.809
that.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:12.209
And so it's all seen as

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:14.039
this endorsement of a military

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.479
government. And Friedman's really

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:17.369
horrified. He's like, I didn't

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.469
ever perceive that going there

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.029
to give my advice.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:22.619
You know, the world expert on

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:24.239
inflation, there's 300%

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:25.049
hyperinflation.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:26.249
Like, of course, I'm going to go and

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:27.929
say, here's what I think should be

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:29.939
done. It doesn't mean I support

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:30.940
Pinochet. So he's

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:33.689
it's a difficult period for him.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:34.980
Why do you think that

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:39.089
slag against him has

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:41.399
been so long lived, though?

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.629
Some of it I just think is

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:43.709
misinformation.

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:45.179
You know, there's there's a belief

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.009
that the the coup was done in

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.899
the service of this particular set

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.220
of economic ideas.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.099
And that's kind of like Naomi

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.199
Klein's in her Shock

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:56.249
Doctrine book.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:57.299
Yeah, there's some dodgy

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.159
documentaries where it's seen that

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.569
like this was all sort of part of a

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:03.209
master plan and that

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:05.129
it was necessary to, you know,

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:06.689
people were so committed to

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:08.219
capitalism that they needed an

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.599
autocratic government to sort of

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:10.829
force it on the country.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:12.659
So, I mean,

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:13.949
it's it's sort of an inverse

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:16.049
narrative of the connection

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.069
between socialism and,

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:19.799
you know, authoritarian regimes.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.269
And so I think it has some power as

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:22.499
being an inversion of that.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:26.369
So I guess my hope is that with

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.169
more information on what happened,

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:29.699
we can have a sort of like a better

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:30.629
understanding of it.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:32.579
And is that kind of that

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.229
connection, is that the origin of

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:36.299
when Friedman really started to talk

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.129
about how when you get economic

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:40.229
freedom, political freedom

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.149
comes traveling along?

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:43.319
Yeah, I think so.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:45.149
What happened was then this is

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.099
like 75, he goes to Chile,

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:48.839
76 is that Nobel Prize.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:50.519
And then the Mount Pelerin Society

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:51.539
has its meeting.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:52.739
I think it's a regional meeting.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:54.750
And like 1981, which again is

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:57.299
interpreted as like this is an

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:58.349
endorsement.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:00.239
And Friedman's really worried

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.999
about that. And he actually gets

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.839
some there is a an economist who

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.309
was in the government and he gets

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:06.669
him disinvited to the meeting

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:08.219
because he doesn't want to feel like

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:09.089
there's a connection.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.159
And at this meeting,

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.019
Chileans are starting to say

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.319
mimic the leftist line that

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:18.419
like we only got capitalism because

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:19.769
we had a strong government and like

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:21.179
we needed that strong government to

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:22.199
get capitalism and freedom.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:23.789
It's like, no, no, no, no, no, no,

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:25.259
no, no. This is not how it works.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:26.909
And he starts kind of pushing back

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:28.349
more.

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:29.489
I think he's also trying to push

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.529
back against Hayek and then talk

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:33.298
more. And I think, you know, for

00:08:33.299 --> 00:08:35.129
most of his career, he's in American

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:37.199
academia and he's taking

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.908
political freedom for granted.

00:08:38.909 --> 00:08:40.798
And he's thinking, my peers

00:08:40.799 --> 00:08:42.329
need to focus more on economic

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:43.619
freedom. And then he gets out into

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.809
the world and it's like, oh,

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:47.369
you know, maybe I need to be talking

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:48.659
about political freedom first

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:50.729
because these two are intertwined.

